  "day_structures_observed": [
    "Mon/Tue/Thu/Fri (labeled Day 1-2, Day 4-5, skipping Wednesday) single-event weekly split following Warm-up -> Stand Throw -> Half Turn -> Full Throw progression \u2014 the most common beginner/intermediate structure, used for both rotational (spin) and glide shot put athletes.",
    "Day 1 Glide / Day 2 Discus split for combo-event athletes doing a shot-put glide technique day and a separate discus technique day within the same week.",
    "Discus Monday+Thursday / Shot Put Tuesday+Friday alternating split for combo athletes who compete in both events (e.g. Caleb Coes, Joseph Herrera, Harlee Shurtliff, Daxtyn Montoya).",
    "Sequential Day 1-4 (no weekday labels), long-form numbered-block ('1a/2a/3a') prose format that explains equipment progressions (stick -> med ball -> full implement) in full sentences rather than short cue phrases \u2014 Eric's earlier, more explanatory template style, seen in Aiden Throws, Pierce Miner 01-03, Anthony Martin, Kaden Throws Plan, Oaks Morley 01.",
    "'Technique Day' / 'Slam Day' alternating pattern (Gemma O'Connell 04, Asher Gavaletz 04, Calum Elder 03) \u2014 lower-volume technical work on Technique Days vs. higher-rep, more competitive-feel 'Slam Day' full throws.",
    "In-season 'Slam Day' / 'Range Day' / 'Distance Day' framing layered onto the Day 1-4 structure for more advanced athletes (Pierce Miner 03-05), where full-throw volume is explicitly tied to a percentage of the athlete's best mark or a competition-simulation intent.",
    "Lifting program is consistently a separate 4-day split (Day 1 Lower Body, Day 2 Upper Body, Day 3 Ab and Jumps, Day 4 Combo) regardless of which throwing-day structure is used, always opening with a shared Dynamic Stretching/Dynamic Warm block."
  ],
  "periodization_notes": "Pierce Miner has the deepest paper trail (programs 01-06) and shows the clearest arc: 01-02 use Eric's earlier, long-form 'numbered block' template (1a/2a/3a rows) with full prose coaching cues for every drill, focused purely on technique acquisition \u2014 Stand Throw stick/med-ball progressions, No Feet variants, Pivot 90/180/360, Big Step, Walking Full, Static Full \u2014 paired with the standard beginner lifting template (Back/Box Squat, DB Snatch, Hamstring Curl, Cable Chest Row, Dead bug/Pallof Press/Box Jumps, Banded Stand Throw finishers). By 03 the athlete is already handling advanced-only drills (South African Drill Progression, Reverse Walk-Through) and the language shifts to competition framing ('Slam Day. Start slow...last couple of throws gear up and push distance', 'Range Day: Keep throws in 80-85% of your best mark'). Programs 04-05 drop the prose format entirely for the terser Day 1-5 weekday grid shared with most other athletes, add 'Up and overs' and combined 'Pivot 90,180,360 Stick drills' rows, and the lifting notes shift to in-season load management ('We are in season \u2014 push a bit but the goal is to throw'). The overall pattern generalizes across athletes: Program 01 is almost always heavy on foundational Stand Throw / No-Feet / basic Pivot drills with simple 3-5 rep prescriptions and a beginner lifting template; by program 02-03 athletes graduate into the 'Plate Push offs' family or full glide sequences with reverse throws, and volumes on full throws climb from 3-5 to 5-8 to 10-12 reps; advanced/veteran programs (Pierce Miner 04-06) introduce competition-day labels, technique refinements specific to that athlete's flaws (from filled-in Notes/Personal Notes columns), and shift lifting toward in-season maintenance language rather than raw strength-building. On the lifting side, the 4-week wave almost always undulates between two tempo variants of the same lift across the month (e.g. Front Squat: Wk1 Pause 70% 5x5, Wk2 82% 5x3, Wk3 70% 3x3, Wk4 75% 5x4; Bench Press: Wk1/3 Normal, Wk2/4 3-second-eccentric or Pause) while %1RM climbs from week to week; bodyweight/core movements instead escalate straight rep counts (Crunches 100->101->102->103, DB Incline Bench '+5lb on your heaviest week'). A recurring Eric-ism is embedding throwing-specific reinforcement directly into the lifting days \u2014 'Stick drill Half turn Drill' (25 reps) and 'Banded Stand Throw and Crunches/Planks' ('have the band in your hand as like throwing a shot put') \u2014 blurring the line between strength work and technical rehearsal.",
